# deploy/standalone/.env -- ONE server's values (gitignored: copy this file to .env on that server and
# fill in every line). Each server has different free ports, addresses and domains, so nothing here
# has a default: compose and up.sh refuse an unset value and name it. up.sh also refuses a door
# without sign-in, a gate or an internal service (LB, node, searxng) that is not on loopback
# (127.x, localhost, [::1]): the public door is the only listener allowed off the box, and it asks
# the jwt gate. Values are plain KEY=VALUE, no quotes, no inline comments.

# ── the model node (one GPU) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The node's image: pin a digest (repo@sha256:...) or a local build's image id (sha256:...), never a moving tag.
EDGE_NODE_IMAGE=
# The server it runs: vllm or faster-whisper (node-engine.<engine>.yml) -- the launch choice's catalog node_engine;
# node-env renders it -- copy it.
EDGE_NODE_ENGINE=
# The node's compose project: one per node on this box (a chat node and a speech node are two). A standalone server
# whose LB and gates are docker-compose.yml's names that file's: vllm-standalone.
EDGE_NODE_PROJECT=
# The container's name -- also its name on EDGE_NODE_NETWORK, where the LB dials it on :8000.
EDGE_NODE_NAME=
# The GPU it runs on: ONE device id as its driver numbers it -- nvidia-smi's index, or ROCm's (ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES)
# (another card may be someone else's).
EDGE_NODE_GPU=
# How the container attaches that GPU: nvidia (Docker's device reservation, node-gpu.nvidia.yml) or rocm (/dev/kfd +
# /dev/dri, node-gpu.rocm.yml). The serving card's hardware type states it (lb/vllm_lb/hardware/types/*.yml
# gpu_runtime); `python -m vllm_lb.fleet node-env` renders this line -- copy it, never choose it by hand.
EDGE_NODE_GPU_RUNTIME=
# The ROCm override only (node-gpu.rocm.yml): the HOST's numeric video and render group ids the container joins to
# reach /dev/kfd and /dev/dri -- per host (`getent group video render`); a server on an NVIDIA card leaves them out.
#EDGE_NODE_VIDEO_GID=
#EDGE_NODE_RENDER_GID=
# The MFU byte-size patch: on or off (node-mfu.on.yml / node-mfu.off.yml) -- on only for a checkpoint whose quant method
# vLLM's MFU model lacks a byte size for (modelopt_mixed). node-env renders it from the catalog -- copy it.
EDGE_NODE_MFU_PATCH=
# loopback host:port the node's OpenAI API is also published on (for checks on the server itself).
EDGE_NODE_PUBLISH=
# OPTIONAL, node-only files -- the node runs on ANOTHER box's docker (e.g. ssh://user@host): up.sh and the applier send
# every docker call there, and EDGE_NODE_PUBLISH is then a private address on that box's link to this one.
#EDGE_NODE_DOCKER_HOST=
# The docker network the node and the LB share (up.sh creates it when missing).
EDGE_NODE_NETWORK=
# The host directory that holds the checkpoint directories (mounted read-only at /models).
EDGE_MODELS_DIR=
# The checkpoint directory under EDGE_MODELS_DIR, and the concurrent requests (seats) the engine takes.
EDGE_NODE_MODEL=
EDGE_NODE_MAX_NUM_SEQS=
# VLLM AND SGLANG (EDGE_NODE_ENGINE=vllm or sglang; node-engine.<engine>.yml, whose compose refuses the node without
# them, naming each): the name the API serves the model as and its context window -- and, vLLM's alone, the GPU memory
# fraction it may take (SGLang sizes its own).
#EDGE_NODE_SERVED_NAME=
#EDGE_NODE_MAX_MODEL_LEN=
#EDGE_NODE_GPU_MEM_UTIL=
# OPTIONAL -- the one line that may be left out (or empty): vLLM's prefill chunk, --max-num-batched-tokens.
# Unset is vLLM's own default for the card; a smaller chunk leaves the KV pool more of the card and prefills
# slower. The fleet file states it per launch (max_num_batched_tokens); node-env renders this line.
#EDGE_NODE_MAX_NUM_BATCHED_TOKENS=
# OPTIONAL -- the KV pool PINNED in bytes (--kv-cache-memory-bytes). Unset: vLLM sizes it from EDGE_NODE_GPU_MEM_UTIL.
# Set, vLLM allocates this whatever the fraction (it still checks the fraction against the card's free memory at
# start): the card's whole KV is the boot log's "--kv-cache-memory=N ... to fully utilize gpu memory". Fleet key:
# kv_cache_memory.
#EDGE_NODE_KV_CACHE_MEMORY=
# OPTIONAL -- a KV tier in the HOST's RAM, GiB (--kv-offloading-size, native backend). Unset: none. Pinned whole at
# start in the host's /dev/shm (the node runs with ipc: host), so it is RAM taken from the box and at most /dev/shm's
# free space, which vLLM checks. Fleet keys: kv_offload_ram_gib, bounded by the box's kv_offload_ram_max_gib.
#EDGE_NODE_KV_OFFLOAD_RAM_GIB=
# OPTIONAL -- the KV cache's element type (--kv-cache-dtype: nvfp4, fp8_e4m3, ...; vLLM's and SGLang's). Unset: the
# engine's own (auto, the model's dtype). One the node's IMAGE accepts -- the fleet refuses any other, naming the list it read out of that
# image (lb/vllm_lb/kv_cache_dtypes.json). Fleet key: kv_cache_dtype.
#EDGE_NODE_KV_CACHE_DTYPE=
# The engine's parsers for this model (vLLM's and SGLang's), each OPTIONAL: its thinking (--reasoning-parser) -- unset passes no flag, for a model
# with no thinking format (a parser would empty `content`) -- and its tool calls (--tool-call-parser, with
# --enable-auto-tool-choice): a CHAT node's, which the fleet requires (a chat client sends tools with tool_choice
# auto); unset for a transcription node, which serves no tools.
#EDGE_NODE_REASONING_PARSER=
#EDGE_NODE_TOOL_CALL_PARSER=
# FASTER-WHISPER AND NEMO ONLY (EDGE_NODE_ENGINE=faster-whisper or nemo; node-engine.<engine>.yml), each OPTIONAL:
# realtime requests queued behind busy seats (the service's MAX_QUEUE_SIZE, with FAIL_FAST_WHEN_BUSY off; unset: a busy
# request is answered 503 at once) and, faster-whisper's alone, CTranslate2's compute type (unset: the image's own).
# Fleet keys: max_queue, compute_type.
#EDGE_NODE_MAX_QUEUE=
#EDGE_NODE_COMPUTE_TYPE=
# The container's limits on this box (the fleet file's box `node_limits`; node-env renders the four -- copy them): its
# memory with no swap (e.g. 24g), CPUs (e.g. 16), processes (e.g. 4096) and the engine's niceness (e.g. 10). The box is
# shared with the LB and the broker: the node runs bounded, never by a default.
EDGE_NODE_MEMORY=
EDGE_NODE_CPUS=
EDGE_NODE_PIDS_LIMIT=
EDGE_NODE_NICE=

# ── how this server's box is named ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The box name the bench runner reports to the panel. It must be the box's name in the LB's fleet file
# (lb/fleet.yml), which also holds its label, hostname, cards and cluster -- the LB reads those there.
EDGE_BOX=

# ── internal services: loopback host:port each is published on ────────────────────────────────────
# The LB (any OpenAI- or Anthropic-compatible client works against it) and its searxng.
EDGE_LB_PUBLISH=
EDGE_SEARXNG_PUBLISH=
# searxng's container name (a shared box may already run one called `searxng`).
EDGE_SEARXNG_CONTAINER=
# What YOUR reverse proxy presents as Host when it forwards to EDGE_LB_PUBLISH -- comma-separated host[:port] (a name
# with no port matches :80/:443: nginx's `proxy_set_header Host $host` sends no port) -- or `none`. The LB refuses
# every other name (lb/vllm_lb/host_guard.py, the DNS-rebinding guard). up.sh derives the LB's LB_ALLOWED_HOSTS from
# this line, EDGE_LB_PUBLISH, the LB's docker names (vllm-lb:8010, lb:8010) and, with the signed-in edge, its doors'
# origins -- so lb/.env must NOT set LB_ALLOWED_HOSTS (up.sh refuses it there).
EDGE_LB_PROXY_HOSTS=

